/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  heatExchanger03_pure_water_plateau
  ==================================

  ONE component, two phases, and the one state a temperature and a pressure
  cannot name.

  A pure fluid at fixed P boils at ONE temperature, Tsat(P).  Its enthalpy
  H(T) is therefore not a smooth curve: it JUMPS by the whole latent heat at
  Tsat.  Ask an exchanger for an outlet whose enthalpy lies inside that jump
  and there is no temperature that answers -- the answer is T = Tsat and a
  VAPOUR FRACTION q = (H - h_L)/(h_V - h_L).  And because (T, P) sit on the
  saturation curve, they do not fix q: the stream must CARRY it, as a pin --
  the one exception to "a stream is fixed by two variables", and the only
  stream that may declare T, P AND a vapour fraction.

  Until 2026-10-05 the exchanger's bisection on H(T) closed onto Tsat and
  published the nearer single phase -- short of its own duty by the
  unboiled fraction's latent heat, at exit 0 (measured on
  `utility02_hitec_csp_heater` at 40 bar: 3.06 kW, 1.5 % of its duty).

  Here (counter-current, A = 2 m^2, U = 1000 W/(m^2.K)):
    * `boilerHX` -- 100 bar superheated steam (700 K) against 40 bar
      feedwater (480 K).  The duty comes from eps-NTU; the steam leaves
      PARTLY CONDENSED and the feedwater PARTLY BOILED, both at their own
      Tsat with a pinned quality.  A boiler and a partial condenser in one
      unit.
    * `trimCooler` -- a `heater` with Q < 0 on the condensing steam.  Its
      inlet is the exchanger's pinned two-phase outlet (the pin is read,
      not re-resolved) and its outlet lands on the plateau again: the
      temperature stays at Tsat and only the quality falls.

  What to read:  the Log's `PURE fluid on its saturation plateau` lines; the
  stream table's vf column (two numbers strictly between 0 and 1 at a T
  that does not move); and the energy balance, where each unit closes to
  the duty it declares.

  STRUCTURAL WITNESS, NOT A VALIDATION.  Every number here comes from this
  case's own water record: an Antoine fit stated for 273-373 K (so Tsat at
  40 and 100 bar is EXTRAPOLATED, and announced), an ideal-gas vapour, and
  the Watson latent heat.  Tsat(40 bar) is the record's 515.6 K, not the
  steam tables' 523.5 K.  What the case pins is the MECHANICS -- the outlet
  sits at Tsat with the quality the first law demands, and the balances
  close -- never the steam properties.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        boilerHX;
        type        heatExchanger;
        inputs      ( steam  feedwater );
        outputs     ( wetSteam  wetFeed );

        operation
        {
            area     2.0 m2;
            U        1000.0 W/m2/K;
            flow     counter;
        }
    }

    {
        name        trimCooler;
        type        heater;
        inputs      ( wetSteam );
        outputs     ( condensate );

        operation
        {
            Q        -500.0 kW;    // removed; lands back on the plateau
        }
    }
);
